 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Comparison",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The bit pairs are weighed from the top down. The first pair that differs, starting at A3/B3, decides the result: A larger there sets QA>B HIGH, B larger sets QA<B HIGH, and the other two outputs go LOW. Only when all four pairs match do the cascade inputs decide the outputs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cascade inputs with equal words",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "With all four bit pairs equal: IA>B alone HIGH gives QA>B; IA<B alone HIGH gives QA<B; IA=B HIGH gives QA=B whatever IA>B and IA<B are. IA>B and IA<B both HIGH with IA=B LOW drives all three outputs LOW; all three cascade inputs LOW drives QA>B and QA<B HIGH and QA=B LOW."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Least significant stage",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The comparator holding the lowest bits needs IA<B and IA>B LOW and IA=B HIGH for a correct result."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Longer words",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "For more than 4 bits, a stage's QA>B, QA=B and QA<B outputs go to the matching IA>B, IA=B and IA<B inputs of the next more significant stage. A 12-bit comparison can be built either as a series chain of three parts or as a two-rank parallel set of four parts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input levels",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The 74HC85 has CMOS input levels; the 74HCT85 has TTL input levels (VIH 2.0 V min, VIL 0.8 V max at VCC = 4.5 V to 5.5 V)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input clamp diodes",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Every input has clamp diodes, so an input fed through a current-limiting resistor can be driven from a voltage above VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Process controllers and servo-motor control are named as uses."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standards",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Meets JEDEC JESD8C (2.7 V to 3.6 V) and JESD7A (2.0 V to 6.0 V)."
  }
 ],
